---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('aftership-email')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-aftership-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-aftership-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-aftership-email');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does AfterShip Tracking provide this checkout recovery flow?",
    "answer": "The cited recovery setup is in AfterShip Email marketing. Tracking shipment notifications have a separate setup; a Tracking installation does not establish marketing event availability."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both products send recovery texts?",
    "answer": "Postscript documents subscriber SMS flows. AfterShip documents Shopify abandoned-checkout SMS on Pro and Enterprise with destination and consent restrictions. Verify current account scope before enabling a second sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should a delivery complaint trigger a recovery offer?",
    "answer": "In this proposed process, service owns the unresolved delivery question. Pause promotional recovery and resume only after the issue and customer intent are reconciled."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the $677 fixture assume?",
    "answer": "At 58% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $284.34. An 8% code removes $54.16 from revenue and reduces gross profit from $392.66 to $338.50 before other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Postscript vs AfterShip Email: who owns recovery and delivery questions?</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">AfterShip marketing recovery and shipment notifications solve different jobs. Compare SMS ownership, checkout eligibility and the service handoff before treating an existing tracking installation as a reason to change recovery tools.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "SMS owner", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Evaluate subscriber cart and checkout SMS with explicit queue cancellation."}, {"label": "Marketing owner", "tool": "AfterShip Email", "reason": "Evaluate checkout email filters and optional SMS separately from Tracking notifications."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Decide whether the missing owner is marketing or service</h2>
<p>A shopper leaving a proposed $677 basket after seeing international shipping charges needs a clear delivery-cost answer. A repeat buyer asking where a previous order is needs service, even if a new $234 basket exists. Give marketing ownership of the new checkout reminder, logistics ownership of the shipping promise, and service ownership of the unresolved shipment. A delivery exception must not become a fabricated scarcity or discount message.</p>
<h2>AfterShip Email: inspect the marketing queue</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440875-how-to-increase-conversions-with-abandoned-checkout-emails">checkout email guide</a> documents minimum checkout value, recent-order filters and configurable delays. Its <a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440894-how-to-enable-abandoned-cart-sms">SMS guide</a> lists Shopify, Pro/Enterprise, consent and US, Canada, Australia or UK recipients. It excludes paid or archived orders and abandonment events before installation. Compare a new checkout with an older event and inspect admission before assuming a migration will replay missed reminders.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/tracking/articles/15441680-set-up-tracking-email-notifications-in-aftership">Tracking notifications</a> have their own admin configuration for post-purchase shipment updates. This does not prove that a delivery exception or return is available as an Email flow condition. Our proposed handoff requires demonstrating the exact event mapping or using an explicit service-maintained suppression process. Record who clears that pause; do not infer resolution from a reminder click.</p>
<h2>Postscript: retain one accountable SMS sender</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">Postscript documents</a> distinct cart-add and checkout-start flows, trigger-specific return links and checkout cancellation on order creation. Inspect those boundaries with a subscriber who advances through both stages. If AfterShip owns email while Postscript owns SMS, assign one operator to reconcile both queues. The cited documentation does not establish automatic cross-product suppression.</p>
<h2>Test delivery and checkout exceptions separately</h2>
<p>Create fixtures just below and above the chosen checkout-value threshold, then place an order during the delay. Check the recent-order window and remaining email/SMS queue. Next, simulate a delivery question on a previous order: service should receive the question and marketing should pause the new basket reminder through the configured mechanism. Release it only when service records resolution and the shopper still has an eligible checkout.</p>
<p>For gift purchases, confirm the actual delivery deadline and unfinished gift details with logistics; do not promise arrival from an email template. For sizing or an unavailable variant, offer assistance or an in-stock alternative without linking to an impossible basket. For a subscription toggle, show cadence and terms. For bundle stacking, financing/BNPL hesitation or a below-MOQ basket, route the specific pricing or payment question to the responsible team before proposing an incentive. Compare mobile newcomers with repeat buyers so a stored address is not mistaken for permission or intent.</p>
<h2>Price the offer independently of the channel</h2>
<p>These proposed basket fixtures keep goods cost fixed. At $677 and 58% gross margin, goods cost is $284.34 and full-price gross profit is $392.66. An 8% code removes $54.16 from revenue, leaving $622.84 revenue and $338.50 gross profit. At $234 and 70% margin, goods cost is $70.20 and gross profit is $163.80. A 12% code removes $28.08, leaving $205.92 revenue and $135.72 gross profit. These are not validated incentive caps. Shipping subsidies, payment fees, returns, platform fees and service effort still need to be deducted.</p>
<h2>Stage the replacement without competing reminders</h2>
<p>These are proposed acceptance checks, not an executed merchant trial. Start with a fresh, identifiable test shopper and channel-specific permission. Record the source event, store, basket, currency, destination URL and each queued message. Advance from browse to cart to checkout, then purchase during the wait. Verify that earlier queues stop and that no further recovery message remains. Repeat after an unsubscribe, stock change, refund and repeat purchase. A refund needs a service decision rather than an automatic restart of recovery.</p>
<p>Choose delays from your buying cycle and staffing coverage; no universal 36-minute or seven-hour ladder is established. Recheck stock, purchase state, permissions, quiet hours and other campaigns before dispatch. Give one operator responsibility for the combined email and SMS calendar. Pause the replaced recovery flow before enabling its successor, preserve configuration for rollback, and inspect the pending queue before resuming either system.</p>
<h2>Measure the decision rather than attributed revenue</h2>
<p>Randomize eligible shoppers into a treatment and a holdout sized for useful precision. Keep sales-assisted orders identifiable and use the same observation window. Compare incremental gross profit after discounts, refunds, message fees and staff cost, then account for platform and implementation costs. A proposed 5% holdout is a starting hypothesis, not a guarantee of adequate power. Platform-attributed purchases alone do not show how many shoppers would have returned anyway.</p>
<p>Product documentation checked November 25, 2026. Request current pricing for the audience, channels, usage, add-ons and implementation scope you actually need; no entry-price or rating ranking is asserted here.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
